/**
 * The Forge simulation: a patch of a galaxy's disk from 1 to 13.8 billion years.
 *
 * Molecular clouds hold the gas. They form stars on their own (Kennicutt–Schmidt: rate ∝ gas^1.4,
 * faster in metal-rich gas), with masses drawn from the initial mass function. The player presses
 * inside a cloud to start a star and holds to feed it: mass sets everything else, so feeding is the
 * era's central choice. Small stars outlive the era and join the disk's steady light; stars of up to
 * eight Suns swell into red giants and leave planetary nebulae and white dwarfs; heavier ones end
 * as supernovae and leave neutron stars or black holes. Pairs make the rest: a white dwarf that
 * steals enough gas from its companion explodes as a Type Ia; two dead stars in orbit spiral
 * together, and two neutron stars make a kilonova. Every death returns gas, richer in metals, and
 * discovers elements according to their real origin.
 */
